WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:09.500
In 1965 in one of the most remote locations
on earth six teenagers were enduring a school

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.950
day much like any other boys their age.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.769
Well, sort of.

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:20.080
Natives of Tongatapu, the main island of the
Kingdom of Tonga – not only were they trapped

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.060
on an itty-bitty idyll in the blue abyss,
they were equally trapped in their strict

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.930
Catholic boarding school.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:31.869
It was a horrid place run by nasty old nuns
who loved nothing more than to hit pupils

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:33.900
over the head with Bibles.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:41.640
As any feisty fifteen-year-old would, one
day, sick of prayers and Latin they decided

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.370
to run away.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:49.510
Come lunchtime the six-strong gang had gathered
on the beach and “borrowed” a boat from

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.340
a grumpy fisherman whom they had a particular
disdain for.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.730
Their plan?

00:00:54.730 --> 00:01:02.190
To somehow navigate their tiny vessel to Fiji
to start a new life, hopefully somewhere with

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.809
less bastard nuns knocking around.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:09.439
They packed some bananas, a few coconuts and
a small gas burner.

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:14.069
Amazingly not one of the teens thought to
pack a map or compass, evidently fancying

00:01:14.069 --> 00:01:15.789
themselves as little Marco Polos.

00:01:15.789 --> 00:01:21.329
Now, I don’t know if you’ve recently checked
the distance between Tonga and Fiji – I

00:01:21.329 --> 00:01:27.659
know it’s been a busy year – but let me
remind you, it’s about 474 miles!

00:01:27.659 --> 00:01:35.049
So yeah, sans map or compass, I don’t think
much of their chances in this case.

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:40.709
To make matters worse they were all exhausted,
from late-night study sessions and early starts.

00:01:40.709 --> 00:01:46.999
So, only a few klicks out from land, they
dropped anchor for the evening and before

00:01:46.999 --> 00:02:09.759
they knew it, the gentle rocking 

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:22.980
of 

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:39.409
the boat sent them 

00:02:39.409 --> 00:03:06.420
all 

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.280
to sleep.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:14.591
When they awoke the weather had turned, and
the violent waves snapped the boat’s sail

00:03:14.591 --> 00:03:21.749
and rudder clean off, leaving the group to
drift helplessly for eight days, without food

00:03:21.749 --> 00:03:23.090
or water.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.650
They tried catching fish.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:31.420
They tried collecting rainwater in their hollowed-out
coconut shells, allowing everyone just one

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.939
sip in the morning and another in the evening.

00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:42.170
With their boat rapidly falling apart it was
only a matter of time before they sank.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:50.659
But mercifully, on the ninth day, they saw
it: a glimpse of a small, deserted island,

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:57.220
only half a mile squared, with steep cliffs,
rocky beaches and a low-lying coral reef.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:04:01.859
They desperately swam ashore, using planks
from the boat for buoyancy.

00:04:01.859 --> 00:04:08.549
Exhausted, starving, thirsty, they collapsed
onto the island: little did they know it at

00:04:08.549 --> 00:04:15.499
the time, but here these naive teens would
spend their next 15 months.

00:04:15.499 --> 00:04:22.500
The boys had drifted an astonishing 200 miles
until they eventually reached the island of

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:23.500
‘Ata.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:31.850
Now ‘Ata was beautiful, but also completely
barren: it had been uninhabited since 1863,

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:34.260
after slavers evacuated it.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:39.419
The incredible tale of these six school boys
was only recently discovered by a Dutch author

00:04:39.419 --> 00:04:45.840
and historian called Rutger Bregman whilst
researching for his own book, called Humankind:

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.620
A Hopeful History.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:54.940
Bregman tracked down and interviewed the survivors,
and the captain who found them: Peter Warner.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:05:01.220
Now Peter was the youngest son of one of the
richest men in Australia, Arthur Warner.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:07.449
Back in the 1930s Arthur Warner ruled over
a vast empire called Electronic Industries,

00:05:07.449 --> 00:05:12.210
which manufactured radios – as the main
source of home entertainment, that was a pretty

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.130
big deal back then.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:19.440
Poor old Peter was under a lot of pressure
to follow in his father’s footsteps.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:27.400
But Peter, much like the Tongan boys, wasn’t
great at the whole work thing, and so apparently,

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:33.009
like all teenage boys seemed to do back then,
ran away to sea in search of adventure.

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:38.070
He spent the next few years sailing all over
the world, visiting Hong Kong, Stockholm,

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:43.280
Shanghai and St. Petersburg, an extended gap
year, if you will.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.199
He promised his dad he would learn something
sensible like accounting, and although it’s

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:53.680
not exactly clear how he spent his time whilst
travelling, we can be pretty sure it wasn’t

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.240
bean counting – more like beer counting.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:02.449
After Peter’s 5 year long holiday he finally
decided to return home, but still went on

00:06:02.449 --> 00:06:05.759
frequent fishing trips for a taste of freedom.

00:06:05.759 --> 00:06:14.440
In the winter of 1966 he took a trip to Tonga,
and on his way home made a little detour.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:21.259
When, lo and behold, what should he stumble
upon but the little old isle of ‘Ata.

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:27.610
However, as Peter passed the island, he noticed
something odd.

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:32.330
Through his binoculars he could see burnt
patches on the cliffs, and thought this was

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:38.389
strange because it was unusual for forest
fires to start spontaneously in this humid

00:06:38.389 --> 00:06:39.509
part of the world.

00:06:39.509 --> 00:06:46.669
Suddenly, he saw a boy emerge from the bushes,
looking full on Rambo, except probably skinnier.

00:06:46.669 --> 00:06:50.820
He was naked, with hair down to his shoulders
and covered in mud.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:54.070
The boy jumped into the water, and started
swimming towards his boat.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:56.979
At this point Peter got a bit worried.

00:06:56.979 --> 00:07:02.770
At the time criminals were sometimes marooned
on desert islands as punishment, and so he

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:08.470
wasn’t sure if things were actually about
to go full on Rambo, or if he was about to

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.409
become somebody’s dinner.

00:07:10.409 --> 00:07:17.520
Instead, a scraggly but well-spoken kid came
on board, and said, in perfect English: “My

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.970
name is Stephen.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:23.289
There are six of us and we reckon we’ve
been here 15 months.”

00:07:23.289 --> 00:07:28.520
Peter still thought better safe than sorry,
and so radioed back to the capital.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:35.430
The operator confirmed he had indeed found
the lost boys, who’d been presumed dead

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.050
for over a year.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:42.510
The families had even held funerals for their
missing sons, assuming they’d drowned at

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:43.510
sea.

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:48.569
Captain Warner and Stephen went ashore to
collect the other boys, and Peter was shocked

00:07:48.569 --> 00:07:49.939
at what he discovered.

00:07:49.939 --> 00:07:56.379
Not only had the boys managed to survive on
the island, but they had actually thrived.

00:07:56.379 --> 00:08:02.169
Even in the sixties most teenagers would have
been lost without spam and mostly black and

00:08:02.169 --> 00:08:06.720
white television, but these were not ordinary
teenagers.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:12.659
Growing up on tropical Tonga they possessed
skills more suited to surviving on a small

00:08:12.659 --> 00:08:13.659
island.

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:19.290
Warner wrote in his memoirs that “the boys
had set up a small commune with a food garden,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:25.851
hollowed-out tree trunks to store rainwater,
a gym with weights, a badminton court, chicken

00:08:25.851 --> 00:08:33.420
pens and a permanent fire, all from handiwork,
an old knife blade, and much determination.”

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:38.060
I’m not sure what’s more impressive, the
fact they had built a badminton court or that

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:42.670
they had any time to play, given they seemed
to be pretty darn busy.

00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:48.310
The six boys had split up into teams of two,
drawing up a strict rota for garden, kitchen

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:53.290
and guard duty, and so were working virtually
all day long.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:59.140
It would be easy for a bunch of teenagers
to bicker and whine all the time, but the

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:05.570
boys had sussed it out - if they ever started
to argue, they had a strict ‘time-out policy’,

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:08.970
where they would spend four hours alone to
cool off.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:13.260
They kept their spirits up by singing and
praying, and one of the boys even managed

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:18.500
to make a guitar from a bit of driftwood,
half a coconut shell and six steel wires taken

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:21.840
from the boat (I told you they were nifty).

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:28.270
They survived by eating fish, coconuts, birds
and bird-eggs, and even drank the blood of

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:31.020
the animals for extra nutrients.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:34.600
Three months in, they also discovered the
ruins of a village from when the island had

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:41.130
been inhabited, and found bananas, chickens
and wild taro, an edible plant with giant,

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:43.190
spinach-like leaves.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:45.750
It wasn’t all smooth-sailing though.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:50.500
They spent months building a raft only for
it to be smashed to pieces by waves crashing

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:52.090
over the coral reef.

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:56.340
One of the boys also slipped off a cliff and
broke his leg.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:00.600
By some miracle the other boys managed to
clamber down the cliff and bring him back

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:06.760
up, where they set his legs using sticks and
leaves, but he was unable to work for several

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:07.760
months.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:13.170
What’s even more remarkable though is what
happened after they got off the island.

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:18.480
Peter took the boys back to Tonga, and just
as they were coming into the harbour, a police

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:19.480
boat arrived.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:26.170
Were they coming to reward Captain Warner,
or safely deliver the boys to their families?

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:27.170
Nope.

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:33.940
They had arrived to arrest the boys for “borrowing”
the grumpy old man’s boat fifteen months

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:34.940
earlier.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:38.961
Turns out grumpy old men don’t just like
to shout and wave their walking sticks; they

00:10:38.961 --> 00:10:40.960
also like to press charges.

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:45.160
The boys were thrown in jail, and at this
point, may well have been wishing they were

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:49.450
back on the island, where they had more space
and probably better food.

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:51.100
Peter had a plan though.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:56.190
He knew that the story of their shipwreck
was perfect Hollywood material, and he could

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.740
make some money by selling the rights to the
story.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:05.030
And that’s exactly what he did, using his
family’s connections to flog them to one

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:08.130
of the biggest TV channels in Australia.

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:13.740
Peter paid the grumpy old man back, and got
the boys released as long as they agreed to

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:15.700
co-operate with the movie.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:21.410
The King of Tonga also rewarded Warner with
the right to trap lobsters and start a fishing

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:23.820
business in the local waters.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:29.040
Warner used his new privilege to hire the
boys to crew his fishing boat (although you

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:32.630
would’ve thought they might want to spend
some time at home for a change).

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:37.820
It was a win-win situation; Peter didn’t
have to go back to boring old accounting,

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:42.290
and the boys didn’t have to go back to boring
old boarding school.

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:47.980
54 years later, this story has made headlines
all over the world, and the boys have since

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:50.930
been dubbed ‘The Real Lord Of The Flies.’

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:56.940
Now, in case you haven’t read Lord of the
Flies, or like the Tongan boys, prefered to

00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:02.100
piss off to Fiji rather than study, let me
give you a quick recap.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:06.980
The book was written by William Golding in
1954, and tells the story of a group of British

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:12.400
schoolboys who are in a plane crash, and end
up stranded on an uninhabited island.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:19.630
At first they are super excited because, you
know, no adults, but soon their tale takes

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:21.250
a dark turn.

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:25.980
They fight over who is going to be leader,
they struggle to keep the signal fire going,

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:31.460
and start to become frightened of a mysterious
monster in the jungle, which they call the

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:32.800
‘beast.’

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:38.120
Some of the boys become obsessed with hunting,
and think that if they sacrifice pigs as tributes

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:42.120
to the ‘beast’, it’ll leave them alone.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:47.090
After a couple of failed attempts they do
eventually manage to kill a pig, and hold

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:49.850
a celebration, where they go full on tribal.

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:54.630
They paint their faces and dance around the
fire whilst chanting “Kill the pig!

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:55.630
Cut its throat!

00:12:55.630 --> 00:13:00.040
Spill her blood!” - which is pretty creepy
for a bunch of seven year olds.

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:07.220
The problem is they get so carried away pretending
to be savages that they actually become savages

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:13.890
- in the dark they become so hysterical they
accidentally mistake one of the other boys,

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:20.140
Simon, for the beast, and beat him before
literally ripping him apart limb from limb.

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:23.570
Did I mention this is a children’s book?

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:25.900
Things only get worse from there.

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:32.010
Civil war breaks out and one of the factions
kills another boy, Piggy, by squishing him

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:33.870
under a boulder.

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:38.100
Pretty gruesome, but in boarding school they
probably call that character building.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:43.610
They let the fire run rampant, almost burning
the whole island down, and they are just ready

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:49.710
to kill another boy, Ralph, when they are
finally saved by a British navy officer who

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:52.060
saw the fire from afar.

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:56.740
The officer comes up to them and simply says
“I should have thought a pack of British

00:13:56.740 --> 00:14:00.310
boys would have put up a better show than
that.”

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:01.310
Quite.

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:08.210
Now this story is often used as a metaphor
for the human condition, a visceral argument

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:12.340
for the innately evil nature of human beings.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:17.520
To be fair to Golding, this was just after
World War Two and the Holocaust, during which

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:23.380
he served as a Lieutenant aboard a Royal Navy
destroyer involved in sinking the Bismark,

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:29.750
so he’d seen, first hand, how wicked human
beings could be to one other.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:34.100
He was also a schoolteacher, but if that’s
how he thought his pupils behaved in their

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:37.280
spare time, you really have to wonder what
the hell he was teaching.

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:43.370
The thing is though, the story of the Tongan
boys was nothing like the book, and so perhaps

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:48.310
it’s unfair to call them ‘the real Lord
of the Flies’ – they were so much better

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:49.310
than that.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:55.250
They didn’t kill each other, quite the opposite-
they were actually kind to each other.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:15:01.400
What happened on ‘Ata was not about survival
of the fittest, but survival of the friendliest.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:07.660
But how did such a hopeful, heart-warming
story go unheard of for so long?

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:12.700
It’s not as if people aren’t interested
in stories about desert island survival – think

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:18.390
about Lost, Shipwrecked, Castaway, or books
like Robinson Crusoe.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:24.279
Well, Bregman, the historian who wrote about
the Tongan boys for his book, has one theory

00:15:24.279 --> 00:15:30.400
– that people are simply not that interested
in stories with happy endings.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:35.450
Bregman argues that stories with pleasant
messages are overlooked in favour of stories

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:41.700
about conflict and trauma, which isn’t hard
to believe when you look at the daily news.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:48.080
Newspapers and TV broadcasts are filled with
disaster, corruption and incompetence – it’s

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:49.680
all rather depressing.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:54.630
Mind you, if the news reported every time
a train doesn’t explode, it’d take longer

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:57.120
than your morning coffee to get through the
headlines.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:02.810
Science says there's a good reason for all
this doom and gloom – humans are fundamentally

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:05.430
wired with a negativity bias.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:10.640
I’ve covered why this is on the channel
not too long ago, but in short, evolution

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:15.430
has programmed our brains to always favour
negative events.

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:20.230
That’s why it’s so important to celebrate
stories like these though.

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:27.350
They provide hope in humanity, which is increasingly
rare in these strange and uncertain times.

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:32.560
Despite the pessimism peddled by Golding in
Lord of The Flies, I like to believe that

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:39.540
when the chips are down, most people are hard-wired
to help rather than harm each other.

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:49.570
And those brave Tongan boys give me some hope
that may just be true.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:54.660
Thanks for watching.

